# EYES — being in the room, and the one row the whole design ordered itself around.
#
# ⚠ *"Looking straight up, sky pixels: 0 (today: all of them)"* is the design's own
# measurement row, and it was written when the roof had no underside. That is why
# EYES is last on the list: until a ceiling existed, a first-person view indoors was
# a picture of the sky through it. The row is the soffit's payoff, and the OUTDOOR
# station is the control that says the instrument can still find sky when there is
# some — without it, "0" would also be what a broken camera reports.

echo --- flat ground
at 0 0 0
key ArrowUp
wait rebuilt

echo --- a house where the author is looking
at 0 -4 90
key H
wait rebuilt

send 27:1
send 40:4

echo --- THE CONTROL: outdoors, looking STRAIGHT UP. This must be sky and nothing else
at 0 -10 90
send 3:0,-20000
step 120
cam
snap s15-eyes-up-outside
frame

echo --- indoors, looking STRAIGHT UP: a ceiling, and no sky at all
at 0 0 90
step 120
cam
snap s15-eyes-up-inside
frame

echo --- indoors, straight DOWN: the floor, and no sky through it either
send 3:0,20000
step 120
snap s15-eyes-down
frame

echo --- indoors, LEVEL: the room from inside a head
send 3:0,-250
step 120
cam
snap s15-eyes-level
frame

echo --- and back to FOLLOW, which fences the pitch again
send 40:1
at 0 0 90
step 150
cam
frame
